{"corrected_text": "Compare and contrast Dickens' and the Brontë sisters' style of writing.

The main feature of Dickens' and the Brontë sisters' style of writing is realism. All these Victorian novelists use very detailed descriptions of places, people and feelings in order to make their novels as realistic as possible. Anyway, Dickens' style is that of sentimental realism, which mixes comedy and pathos, while the Brontë sisters' realism is full of Romantic and Gothic elements and it's widely influenced by the desolate English Moors, where they grew up.

In addition, Dickens, as well as Charlotte and Emily Brontë in some of their novels, quite frequently uses first-person narration. For example, in "David Copperfield", Dickens's most autobiographical novel, the story is told through a first-person narration from the point of view of the protagonist as an adult. Charlotte Brontë uses the same technique in Jane Eyre, her masterpiece. Finally, in all cases, the stylistic choices are closely linked with the themes they deal with, which always concerned social issues."}

{"corrected_text": "Dickens and the Bronte sisters wrote in very different ways. Dickens’s style is full of sentimental realism: he describes reality with a sentimental tone and he mixes comedy and pathos. Conversely, the Bronte sisters are influenced by the preceding Romantic  works, and their novels contain Gothic elements such as ghosts and a dark ominous atmosphere.

If  their themes are considered, it is immediately clear that Dickens builds an extraordinary image of Victorian society, with a wide range of characters and stories  which reveal the conditions of children and the poor; whereas the Brontes’ novels contain the Byronic hero, a male figure with a dark past who wants to find some revenge, in the case of Heatcliff in "Wuthering Heights"  or forgiveness in the case of Mr Rochester in "Jane Eyre". In contrast with Dickens, the Bronte sisters deal with love and psychologically complex women as protagonists.
